id: k8s.events
kind: kubernetes
title: Events
aliases: kubectl get events, cluster events
tags: operations, diagnostics
questions:
  - a pod stays Pending, how do you find out why?
invariant: Events are short-lived records of what controllers did and why, including scheduling and probe failures.
answer_30s: Events are the scheduler's and kubelet's own explanations: insufficient CPU, unbound PVC, failed probe, image pull error -- visible for a limited retention window.
answer_2m: |
  This is the highest-value diagnostic surface in Kubernetes and the one
  people forget, because the reason for a stuck Pod is almost never in its
  logs: the container never started. Sorting by timestamp turns the tail of
  events into a timeline of the incident (image pulled, probe failed, Pod
  evicted, node NotReady). Retention is short (minutes to an hour by default on
  many distributions), so for post-mortems they must be exported -- which is the
  answer to "how would you debug this tomorrow?".
when_to_use:
  - any Pending, CrashLooping, evicted or unschedulable object
commands:
  - kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp => the incident timeline, oldest to newest
  - kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning => only the failures, across namespaces
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => the tail of that Pod's events, with the messages inline
failure_modes:
  - events expired before anyone looked
  - reading only pod logs for a Pod whose container never started
related:
  - k8s.describe
  - k8s.scheduler
  - k8s.debugging-workflow
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_events/
